## 🟥 PART 3 — AUTONOMOUS GOVERNANCE ENGINE (📋 DESIGNED, PENDING BUILD)

### Modules

| Module | Purpose |
|---|---|
| **Agent Registry** | Every agent registered with ID, version, status, capabilities, permissions, health, heartbeat |
| **Agent Identity** | Unique identity determining access rights, tool permissions, data access |
| **Agent Gateway** | All agent-to-agent communication passes through: auth, authz, routing, rate limits, validation |
| **Permission System** | Capability-based access control (Sentinel: READ_METRICS; Executor: CALL_TOOLS; etc.) |
| **Memory Bank** | Three types: Episodic (what happened), Semantic (what the institution knows), Procedural (what works) |
| **Institutional Learning** | Post-incident: outcome → memory update → pattern extraction → future recommendation |
| **Prediction Engine** | Linear regression on metric trends → predicted time to threshold → preventive incidents |
| **Preventive Action** | Prediction → risk assessment → preventive plan → approval → action → verification |
| **Security/Guardrails** | Treat external data as untrusted; protect against prompt injection, malicious log instructions |
| **Observability** | OpenTelemetry-compliant traces; every agent action produces audit entries |
| **Audit Trail** | Complete timestamp-level recording of every operation in the system |

### Memory System Design

**Episodic Memory** (What happened?):
```
Incident #19482 → Root cause: DB overload → Action: Scale DB → Outcome: Success
```

**Semantic Memory** (What does the institution know?):
```
"Database connection exhaustion commonly occurs after deployment pattern X."
```

**Procedural Memory** (What works?):
```
"For database overload, scaling connection capacity succeeded in 87% of similar incidents."
```

